    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d927">

                <pb facs="1026" n="1016" sameAs="frus:frus1944v04/pg1016"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to President
                        Roosevelt </hi><anchor n="80" xml:id="d927-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-10-15">15 October
                        1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Personal for the President. Stalin was in high mood yesterday. He paid Churchill
                    an unusual and significant compliment by attending the Ballet and Red Army
                    concert at the Opera House. I understand Stalin has not been seen at the Opera
                    since the war.</p>
                <p>Churchill and Stalin received a tremendous ovation from the audience. In the long
                    entr’acte Stalin entertained at a very small supper for Churchill, Eden and
                    Brooke including Kathleen<anchor n="81" xml:id="d927-f2"/> and myself. He made
                    good humored fun of all of us. Kathleen was the only woman in the official box
                    and at the supper. Stalin is showing on every occasion his appreciation to
                    Churchill for his visit.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d927-f1" n="80" type="source" xml:id="d927-f1-content">
                <p>Copy of telegram obtained from Franklin D. Roosevelt Library, Hyde Park, N.Y. A
                    paraphrase of this telegram is in the Department files under
                    741.6111/10–1544.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d927-f2" n="81" xml:id="d927-f2-content">
                <p>Daughter of Ambassador Harriman.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
